```{r, include = FALSE}
knitr::opts_chunk$set(collapse = TRUE, comment = "#>", fig.width = 7, fig.height = 4.2, dpi = 72,
                      fig.path = "figures/traj-", message = TRUE)
options(width = 100, tibble.width = 100, tibble.print_max = 12)
```

> **About this vignette.** All data are simulated with `wgh_simulate()`. The
> results show how the methods work and resemble the published patterns,
> but they are not the published findings and are not research results.
> The functions run in development mode (`wgh_config("dev")`).

Children with overweight or obesity before puberty are often taller than
their peers and then lose that advantage during adolescence. This vignette
reproduces the longitudinal analysis of that pattern (Ho et al. 2026) [1]:
assigning each child a BMI category at a prepubertal index age, comparing
height-for-age between the categories at milestone ages, and modelling the
whole trajectory with a mixed model. It ends with the flow that does it in
one call. The vignette is precomputed.

```{r}
library(WeGrowHealthR)
wgh_config("dev")
v <- wgh_simulate(30000, seed = 2026)$visits
```

## 1. The analytic sample

Trajectories need repeated visits. `wgh_sample_flow()` applies the
restrictions in order and records how many children each step removes.

```{r}
fl <- wgh_sample_flow(v, "Ages 5 to 18" = ~ age >= 5 & age <= 18.5,
                      "At least 2 visits" = wgh_min_visits(2))
fl
```

The restricted data are in `fl$data`.

## 2. Exposure at a prepubertal index age

`wgh_index_exposure()` picks one visit per child in an index window and
attaches its value to every visit of that child. The defaults follow the
study: the earliest visit from 5 years and at or before 11 years in boys
and 9 years in girls (`max_age`), so the exposure is measured before most
children enter puberty. `categories` turns the value into groups, here the
WHO BMI categories without a separate thinness group.

```{r}
ix <- wgh_index_exposure(fl$data, "baz", max_age = c(Male = 11, Female = 9),
                         categories = function(b, a) wgh_bmi_category(b, a, thinness = FALSE))
ix$index_cat <- droplevels(ix$index_cat)
first <- ix[!duplicated(ix$id), ]
table(first$sex, first$index_cat)
```

`index_value` holds the index BAZ, `index_age` the age at the index visit and
`index_cat` the category. Each child keeps the same values at all visits.

## 3. Comparing groups at milestone ages

`wgh_compare_groups()` compares an outcome between groups in windows around
stated ages (`ages`, with half-width `window`). It runs a Kruskal-Wallis test
across all groups and Dunn pairwise tests with Holm adjustment.

```{r}
kw <- wgh_compare_groups(ix, "haz", "index_cat", ages = c(8, 10, 12, 14, 16), by = "sex")
kw$overall
head(kw$pairwise)
```

```{r trajectories, fig.alt = "HAZ by age for each prepubertal BMI category, with p-values at milestone ages", fig.height = 4.4}
wgh_plot_trajectories(ix, "haz", "index_cat", milestones = c(8, 10, 12, 14, 16))
```

Before puberty, children with obesity have the highest height-for-age. The
curves converge in adolescence, and in girls the order even reverses: by 16
years girls who had obesity before puberty are the shortest group.
`wgh_plot_trajectories()` takes the same display options as every plot, for
example `show_ci = FALSE` or `legend = "right"`.

## 4. A mixed model of the trajectory

The group comparisons use cross-sections. A linear mixed model uses every
visit of every child. `wgh_fit_lmm()` fits HAZ with a natural spline of age
interacting with the index category, a fixed site effect, and a random
intercept and slope for age within child (`random = "(1 + age_c | id)"`,
with age centred at 10 years).

```{r}
girls <- ix[ix$sex == "Female", ]
fit_g <- wgh_fit_lmm(girls, haz ~ splines::ns(age, df = 3) * index_cat + site)
wgh_fit_stats(fit_g)
```

The marginal R2 is the variance explained by the fixed effects and the
conditional R2 adds the child random effects. A large difference between
them, and a high ICC, means most of the variation in HAZ is stable
differences between children, as expected for height.

`wgh_contrasts()` reads the model at stated ages and compares categories
pairwise (with `emmeans`):

```{r}
ct <- wgh_contrasts(fit_g, "index_cat", ages = c(8, 12, 16))
ct$contrasts
```

Each row is a difference in mean HAZ between two categories at one age,
with a Holm-adjusted p-value. In girls, those with obesity before puberty
are taller than those with normal BMI at 8 years, the advantage shrinks by
12 years, and it turns into a deficit by 16 years. This is the pattern of
earlier puberty and an earlier end of growth.

## 5. Changes in BMI category

`wgh_exposure_change()` compares each child's category at two ages, to see
how stable the exposure is.

```{r}
ch <- wgh_exposure_change(fl$data, ages = c(7, 10))
table(ch$sex, ch$change)
```

## 6. The whole study in one call

```{r}
rep <- wgh_flow_index_trajectory(v)
rep
rep$tables$contrasts
```

```{r flow-height, fig.alt = "Height by age for each prepubertal BMI category", fig.height = 4.4}
plot(rep, "fig_height_trajectories", title = "Height trajectories by prepubertal BMI category")
```

The report also contains a sensitivity analysis restricted to children with
at least four visits (`rep$tables$sensitivity_4plus_visits`), which checks
that the result does not depend on children seen only twice.

## Where to go next

* Timing of the pubertal spurt explains why the advantage disappears. See
  `vignette("growth-curves")`.
* `wgh_mediation()` estimates how much of an effect passes through a
  mediator, and `wgh_exposure_window()` defines exposures in a window before
  an outcome without leakage.

